WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.770
-Our first guest tonight is an
Emmy- and Golden Globe-nominated

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.138
actress, writer, and producer.

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:08.140
You know her from her critically
acclaimed show "Insecure."

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.010
The first two episodes of
her new show, "Rap [Bleep]",

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.746
begin streaming
on HBO Max July 21st.

00:00:13.746 --> 00:00:17.050
She also stars in "Vengeance,"
which is in theaters July 29th.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.153
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Issa Rae, everybody!

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:22.956
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:37.637 --> 00:00:39.772
-Welcome back, Issa!
-Thank you.

00:00:39.772 --> 00:00:41.407
I'm so happy to be back.

00:00:41.407 --> 00:00:43.743
-I've had the pleasure
of interviewing in person.

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:45.745
I also -- You were very kind
to do a Zoom interview

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:46.780
during the pandemic.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:47.980
-Yes.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.783
-And of all the people
I interviewed on Zoom,

00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:53.920
you had the longest
and loudest doorbell ring.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.557
-It was already embarrassing.

00:00:57.557 --> 00:00:59.825
I have one of
those old-school -- It goes --

00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:01.527
Y'all want to hear it?
It's...

00:01:01.527 --> 00:01:04.363
♪ Da, da, da, da ♪

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.167
♪ Da, da, da, da ♪

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:09.836
-And it was definitely --
You know, it was

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:13.005
the time of the pandemic where
UPS was just coming all day.

00:01:13.005 --> 00:01:14.807
And I think you were
trying to tough it out.

00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:15.808
You were like, "You know what?

00:01:15.808 --> 00:01:16.976
They'll just leave it there."

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:18.678
And I think we heard
the full doorbell ring

00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:19.979
three or four times.

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:23.282
-Yeah, you called it.
I was very package-popular.

00:01:23.282 --> 00:01:24.918
So sorry that
I got lots of packages.

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:26.386
-You did say you were
going to replace it.

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:29.155
That was your promise to us.
Did you?

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.190
-I didn't,
but I let the battery die.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:33.726
So now people just knock, like,
the old-fashioned way.

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:34.994
-That's
really -- You thought ahead.

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:36.962
-I did, yeah.
I did that on purpose.

00:01:36.962 --> 00:01:38.264
I planned that.

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:41.700
-Congratulations on your
many Emmy nominations.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.604
How did you find out that
you were nominated this year?

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:47.306
-You know, like,
it's nerve-racking to know that

00:01:47.306 --> 00:01:48.474
the nominations are coming up.

00:01:48.474 --> 00:01:50.109
And I accidentally
found out that morning.

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:51.577
And I was like, "Oh, man!"

00:01:51.577 --> 00:01:53.045
And then
I just kept myself busy.

00:01:53.045 --> 00:01:54.980
And I was
in the middle of a meeting,

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.449
and then my phone
kept going off.

00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:59.686
And I let out like a...
[ Squeals ]

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:01.321
And, literally,
the meeting stopped,

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:02.755
and they were like,
"Do you want to elaborate?"

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:04.089
And I was like, "Well, no,

00:02:04.089 --> 00:02:05.691
we were talking about
important business right now."

00:02:05.691 --> 00:02:09.262
And then they found out later
and congratulated me.

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:11.363
-It must feel special --
extra special --

00:02:11.363 --> 00:02:15.034
because "Insecure" wrapped up an
incredible five-season run,

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:17.937
and this was, in a way,
its last chance to get honored.

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:19.005
And it was really cool
that people remembered it.

00:02:19.005 --> 00:02:20.073
-That part.

00:02:20.073 --> 00:02:22.374
Like, it was --
We came out in December,

00:02:22.374 --> 00:02:24.676
and, usually,
you know, the academy --

00:02:24.676 --> 00:02:26.980
There's so many great shows,
and people will forget.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:30.416
And so it was just nice to get a
nice -- like, a nod going out.

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:32.185
And, you know, our
cinematographer was nominated,

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:34.453
Ava Berkofsky,
and our editor, Nena Erb.

00:02:34.453 --> 00:02:36.489
So it was just nice
to be remembered.

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:38.891
And hopefully it encourages
people to go watch it.

00:02:38.891 --> 00:02:41.794
-I do think, as a show,
it's going to endure, really,

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:42.928
for a long time because

00:02:42.928 --> 00:02:44.330
it is such a
wonderful show about friendship.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:47.333
And I think that,
like, that does not date badly,

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:48.768
because people --
I feel like even though

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:50.469
the world changes,
the way people are friends

00:02:50.469 --> 00:02:51.538
is very consistent.

00:02:51.538 --> 00:02:53.439
-Friendship is not dated.
-It is not dated.

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:57.309
It stays in style.
-That's beautiful, actually.

00:02:57.309 --> 00:02:58.777
-You also --
This is very exciting.

00:02:58.777 --> 00:03:00.513
You were working on
your Emmy day.

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:01.781
-I was.

00:03:01.781 --> 00:03:04.884
I took a shift at my
local coffee shop, Hilltop.

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:06.385
-Yeah.
-But that was already planned.

00:03:06.385 --> 00:03:08.087
-You're a part owner.
-I am, yes.

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:10.990
-Yeah. So when you say,
"My local coffee shop,"

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:12.225
you mean...

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:13.926
-Literally my local coffee shop.

00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:15.662
It's down the street
from my house.

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:17.696
-Yeah, 'cause when I say --
Oh never mind.

00:03:17.696 --> 00:03:21.534
People like you don't understand
us small people, who...

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:24.036
who just frequent them,
exchange money for coffee.

00:03:24.036 --> 00:03:25.270
-Oh, okay.

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:27.940
-But you look like
you know what you're doing.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.576
-Yeah, I'm glad I look that way,
because, you know,

00:03:30.576 --> 00:03:32.578
I was supposed to --
I was supposed to, like,

00:03:32.578 --> 00:03:33.713
make drinks for people.

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:35.248
That's what a barista does.

00:03:35.248 --> 00:03:37.416
And I was --

00:03:37.416 --> 00:03:39.552
I was supposed to have
a training for at least

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:42.422
10 to 15 minutes,
but I was late for my shift.

00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:44.157
-Okay.

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:45.157
-And so they didn't
get to train me,

00:03:45.157 --> 00:03:46.225
but they were like, "It's okay.

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:47.960
You'll just work the register."

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:49.895
So I was like,
"Oh, okay, I got that."

00:03:49.895 --> 00:03:51.230
But there was
a really long line.

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.132
Shout-out to everybody who came.

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:56.002
And, so, I was
kind of slow on the register.

00:03:56.002 --> 00:04:00.239
And, so, they let me get about
like five customers in,

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:01.473
and then they were like,
"You know, we're just --

00:04:01.473 --> 00:04:02.809
We're going to
have you call names.

00:04:02.809 --> 00:04:04.444
Call names out."

00:04:04.444 --> 00:04:06.112
So that's what I did
for the rest of the time.

00:04:06.112 --> 00:04:07.680
I just --
But I was a great name-caller.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.115
-Wow.
-Like, I was built for that.

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:12.551
-So it does seem like you were
demoted twice in one shift.

00:04:12.551 --> 00:04:14.353
-I was.

00:04:14.353 --> 00:04:15.855
Heavily demoted.
But you know what?

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:18.624
You try it.
-Yeah, no.

00:04:18.624 --> 00:04:19.992
[ Laughter ]

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:21.994
-But respect to the staff.

00:04:21.994 --> 00:04:23.696
They were so patient,
because there were a lot of

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:26.065
orders to fill,
and I was trash, so...

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:29.068
-It does seem like an
episode of "Undercover Boss"

00:04:29.068 --> 00:04:30.469
where they figure it out
right away.

00:04:30.469 --> 00:04:34.340
They're like, "I think that lady
making coffee is our boss."

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:37.042
-"I think she's in on it."
Yeah.

00:04:37.042 --> 00:04:38.878
-This was --
So, this is in Inglewood.

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:40.112
-Yeah, it is.
-Your coffee shop.

00:04:40.112 --> 00:04:43.449
And you were honored recently.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:44.883
They gave you
the key to the city.

00:04:44.883 --> 00:04:45.885
-They did.

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:47.353
-And there's
the key to the city.

00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:51.057
-Yes. Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:52.325
-It's funny.

00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:54.393
Like, when I was little,
I definitely assumed, like,

00:04:54.393 --> 00:04:56.062
it was a functional key,

00:04:56.062 --> 00:04:57.330
like, when you heard
"key to the city."

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:58.764
-It should be, honestly.
-Yeah.

00:04:58.764 --> 00:05:02.101
-It should be a key
that just opens up, you know,

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:04.670
people's cars, offices.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.839
-At the very
least, municipal buildings,

00:05:06.839 --> 00:05:08.808
like library after hours,
you know?

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:10.242
-I would love that.
-Yeah.

00:05:10.242 --> 00:05:11.944
-But it doesn't --
It's just a big key.

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:13.612
Like, it's a big plastic key.

00:05:13.612 --> 00:05:15.380
And, unfortunately,
somebody was like,

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:18.050
"Oh, you'll get, like,
all this access and, you know,

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:19.418
maybe you won't have to pay
parking tickets."

00:05:19.418 --> 00:05:21.087
But it doesn't do any of that.
It just --

00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:23.589
-What do you even do with it?
Like, where is the key now?

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:24.857
-It's actually in
my office, propped up.

00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:25.858
-Alright.

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:27.860
-And they sent it --

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:29.728
Blessings for them,
but they sent it all lopsided,

00:05:29.728 --> 00:05:32.631
so it's, like, a diagonal key.

00:05:32.631 --> 00:05:34.533
So I have to reframe it, and
it will be beautiful after that.

00:05:34.533 --> 00:05:36.001
-Okay, gotcha.

00:05:36.001 --> 00:05:38.070
-But, you know, it's a great --
It's a conversation starter.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:39.238
Like, "What is that?"

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:42.808
"Oh, it's the --
You mean my key to the city?"

00:05:42.808 --> 00:05:44.843
-That seems like
a very aggressive way

00:05:44.843 --> 00:05:46.745
to start a conversation.

00:05:46.745 --> 00:05:48.748
"What is that?"

00:05:48.748 --> 00:05:51.150
I have a lot more to
ask you about your new show.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.086
We'll be right back
with more from Issa Rae.

